{"parentUuid":"ce166591-4cbe-4339-a9f5-ac5747349c5b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgGBucA7tPsEb9b3eKCcY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01K9dtpNVLuFep1FfGz8JHrp","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"git add -A && git -c commit.gpgsign=false commit -q -F - <<'MSG'\nfix(watch): wait on the LOCK, not the process, and only when it is contended\n\nCI caught two defects in my first version of the COR-2175 fix. Both were mine.\n\n1. It waited on the PROCESS, by re-execing os.Executable(). Inside a test\n   binary that is the TEST BINARY — spawned, and then waited on. 36 tests stub\n   memoryWorkerLaunch precisely so nothing spawns; my new seam bypassed every\n   one of them. The launch now stays behind memoryWorkerLaunch and the tick\n   waits for the brain write lock instead, which is the resource actually in\n   contention and stops the moment the worker lets go.\n\n2. The wait treated EVERY lock error as \"still held\". A brain root that cannot\n   be locked for another reason — a rejected symlink, permissions — will still\n   fail in 90 seconds, so each such test sat through the entire budget. That\n   is what pushed `test (ubuntu-latest)` from its normal runtime to 30m42s and\n   over the job cap: the failure was a TIMEOUT, not an assertion. Only\n   brain_locked is waited out now; anything else returns at once. The suite is\n   back to 322s locally.\n\nTwo of the tests I wrote for this were also wrong, and I only found them by\nweakening:\n\n  - the ordering test measured elapsed time AFTER joining the goroutine that\n    released the lock, so it was >= the sleep no matter what settle did. It\n    passed against a settle that returned immediately. Measured before the\n    join now.\n  - the non-contention test used a MISSING directory, which the lock simply\n    creates and succeeds on, so it exercised nothing — it passed against the\n    exact bug it was written for. It uses a symlinked root now, which\n    rejectSymlinkedBrainRoot genuinely refuses, and weakening the filter makes\n    it fail after 60s as it should.\n\nVerified by weakening, five guards: removing the settle call, making settle\nreturn immediately, dropping the contention filter, blanking the log stamp,\nand removing the cross-branch half of the chunk-reuse decision. All red, all\nrestored.\n\nFull internal/cli suite green in 322s, -race green, go vet ./... clean.\nMSG\ngit push origin HEAD:fix/distill-pipeline 2>&1 | grep -viE \"^remote:|vulnerab|dependabot|^\\[entire\\]|cannot lock|another git\" | tail -2","description":"Push the corrected fix"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":769,"cache_read_input_tokens":896508,"output_tokens":960,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":769,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":960,"cache_read_input_tokens":896508,"cache_creation_input_tokens":769,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":769},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01K9dtpNVLuFep1FfGz8JHrp":{"command":"cd /Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-distill-fix && git add -A && git -c commit.gpgsign=false commit -q -F - <<'MSG'\nfix(watch): wait on the LOCK, not the process, and only when it is contended\n\nCI caught two defects in my first version of the COR-2175 fix. Both were mine.\n\n1. It waited on the PROCESS, by re-execing os.Executable(). Inside a test\n   binary that is the TEST BINARY — spawned, and then waited on. 36 tests stub\n   memoryWorkerLaunch precisely so nothing spawns; my new seam bypassed every\n   one of them. The launch now stays behind memoryWorkerLaunch and the tick\n   waits for the brain write lock instead, which is the resource actually in\n   contention and stops the moment the worker lets go.\n\n2. The wait treated EVERY lock error as \"still held\". A brain root that cannot\n   be locked for another reason — a rejected symlink, permissions — will still\n   fail in 90 seconds, so each such test sat through the entire budget. That\n   is what pushed `test (ubuntu-latest)` from its normal runtime to 30m42s and\n   over the job cap: the failure was a TIMEOUT, not an assertion. Only\n   brain_locked is waited out now; anything else returns at once. The suite is\n   back to 322s locally.\n\nTwo of the tests I wrote for this were also wrong, and I only found them by\nweakening:\n\n  - the ordering test measured elapsed time AFTER joining the goroutine that\n    released the lock, so it was >= the sleep no matter what settle did. It\n    passed against a settle that returned immediately. Measured before the\n    join now.\n  - the non-contention test used a MISSING directory, which the lock simply\n    creates and succeeds on, so it exercised nothing — it passed against the\n    exact bug it was written for. It uses a symlinked root now, which\n    rejectSymlinkedBrainRoot genuinely refuses, and weakening the filter makes\n    it fail after 60s as it should.\n\nVerified by weakening, five guards: removing the settle call, making settle\nreturn immediately, dropping the contention filter, blanking the log stamp,\nand removing the cross-branch half of the chunk-reuse decision. All red, all\nrestored.\n\nFull internal/cli suite green in 322s, -race green, go vet ./... clean.\nMSG\ngit push origin HEAD:fix/distill-pipeline 2>&1 | grep -viE \"^remote:|vulnerab|dependabot|^\\[entire\\]|cannot lock|another git\" | tail -2","description":"Push the corrected fix"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_01K9dtpNVLuFep1FfGz8JHrp":{"cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv