{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","hash":"4b8bd9022e1f1d78333fcd370681ee79d1655b97a52c14e0e30360c64fe2dca4","message":{"role":"system","content":"<total_tokens>15000000 tokens left</total_tokens>"}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","hash":"b35c58bd2174849568a2427ebb00cd4058da9b5c4ee8400f68503e69ac3a6dcd","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Paused. Here's the whole thing.\n\n## Where it was\n\nOne file, one line: **`internal/cli/facts_locus.go:211`** in `entire-brain`.\n\n```go\nvar locusIdentifierPattern = regexp.MustCompile(\n  `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*(?:[./][A-Za-z0-9_]+)+|...`)\n```\n\nLook at the character classes: `[A-Za-z0-9_]`. **No hyphen anywhere.**\n\nThis regex is what `factLocus(text)` runs over a fact's text to pull out the code it's about — the symbols, packages and **file paths**. Those become the fact's `locus`, which is what the brain uses to tie a fact to a file.\n\n## Why it produced ashtom's exact symptom\n\nWalk `website/scripts/pre-build.ts` through it:\n\n1. starts at `w`, eats `website`\n2. `(?:[./][A-Za-z0-9_]+)+` eats `/scripts`, then `/pre`\n3. hits `-` — **not in any class** — match ends → token #1 = `website/scripts/pre`\n4. regex restarts after the hyphen → `build` + `.ts` → token #2 = `build.ts`\n\nThe real path never appears. That's his \"also yields `website/scripts/pre` and `build.ts`\", precisely.\n\nAnd his follow-up — *\"the fragments still appear next to the correct paths, because the extractor always produces them\"* — is because `factLocus` has **two** passes: backtick spans are taken whole, *then* the regex sweeps the entire text again. Backticks added the correct token; the sweep added the fragments beside it. He diagnosed that correctly from the outside.\n\n## The part he didn't see, which is worse\n\nA fragment isn't just noise — it's a *plausible-looking* path that resolves to nothing. And `factLocusDrift` (the thing that tells an agent \"this fact is stale, the file is gone\") can't catch it. Its filter is:\n\n```go\nfunc locusTokenIsFilePath(tok string) bool {\n    if !strings.Contains(tok, \"/\") { return false }   // kills \"build.ts\"\n    return locusFileExtPattern.MatchString(tok)        // kills \"website/scripts/pre\"\n}\n```\n\nNeeds a slash **and** an extension. `website/scripts/pre` has no extension. `build.ts` has no slash. Neither reaches the `os.Stat`. **So any fact about a hyphenated file could never be reported as drifted — delete the file and the stale fact survives forever.**\n\nWhile I was in there I found the same defect's twin: the regex has no leading `.`, and `add()` did `strings.Trim(tok, \"`.,;:()[]{}'\\\"\")` — trimming that cutset from *both* ends. So `.github/workflows/ci.yml` got stored as `github/workflows/ci.yml`. That one fails the *opposite* way: it *does* pass `locusTokenIsFilePath`, resolves nowhere, and gets flagged as drifted on every single recall.\n\n## How I fixed it\n\nSegments became `[A-Za-z0-9_]+(?:-[A-Za-z0-9_]+)*`, with an optional leading dot:\n\n```go\n`\\.?[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*(?:-[A-Za-z0-9_]+)*(?:[./][A-Za-z0-9_]+(?:-[A-Za-z0-9_]+)*)+|...`\n```\n\nTwo deliberate constraints so this doesn't over-fire:\n- the hyphen must be **interior** (`-` followed by more name), so a trailing dash stays out of the token\n- the first alternative still **requires** a `.` or `/`, which is what keeps `state-of-the-art` out of the locus\n\nAnd `add()` now trims leading and trailing punctuation with **separate cutsets**, so a leading dot survives.\n\n```\n                              before                            after\nwebsite/scripts/pre-build.ts  [website/scripts/pre, build.ts]   [website/scripts/pre-build.ts]\n.github/workflows/ci-win.yml  [github/workflows/ci, win.yml]    [.github/workflows/ci-win.yml]\nstate-of-the-art prose        []                                []\n```\n\n**No migration.** `reclassify` already rewrites `Locus` whenever it differs from what the text derives (`facts_locus.go:158`), so existing facts shed their fragments on the next pass.\n\n## How I know it's actually fixed\n\nI wrote the test first and ran it against unpatched code — it failed with his exact output, backticked case included. Then **verified by weakening**: I reverted each of the three guards one at a time and confirmed the tests go red each time, then restored all three.\n\n```\nW1 drop interior-hyphen groups  → 5 subtests RED\nW2 drop leading dot             → 2 subtests RED\nW3 restore symmetric trim       → 2 subtests RED\nrestored                        → ok\n```\n\nFull `internal/cli` green (211s), build + vet clean, **29 CI checks pass**.\n\nAnd the live proof: I recorded a fact about this with your *currently installed* brain, and its locus came back\n`[..., 'build.ts', 'github/workflows/ci.yml', 'website/scripts/pre', 'website/scripts/pre-build.ts']` — the bug, reproducing in your own brain right now.\n\n→ **PR [#410](https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/pull/410)**, trail **[#324](https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/324)**.\n\n---\n\nOne side-discovery worth your attention, since it'll bite anyone else: `entire trail create` has been failing with a bare `422 validation_error`. It's not the server — the cell's schema wants **`branch_name` / `branch_action`** (snake_case) with `additionalProperties: false`, and the installed CLI posts `branchName` / `branchAction`. Every field reads as unknown, so the body is rejected whole. Same class as the finding-404 gotcha: a stale local binary failing with a status code that blames your request. I got trail #324 created by posting snake_case directly."}]}}
