{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","hash":"bdfcf1641394881302c4b2f63c79da54a9d351e6b7ba72e90d6d1e06ed71e14b","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All eight conflicts are fixed, verified and pushed. Merging is blocked again — that's the one thing I can't do without you.\n\n## Conflicts fixed — all 8, each verified by weakening\n\n| PR | what it actually needed |\n|---|---|\n| **#373** | Both sides added a function at the same spot. Kept both; dropped `reportSetupComponentFailures` (main's `setupComponentFailureDetails` supersedes it, with a `seenHints` dedup). |\n| **#372** | Import-block conflicts only; pruned `tui` once the compiler proved it dead. |\n| **#348** | `publish.go` → main (it **retired** `ENTIRE_BRAIN_ALLOW_HOSTED` in `c6c3eed3`; I checked the history, not the diff). Then its own guard caught **8 hardcoded verbs** main had added since. |\n| **#349** | Three hunks taking main's *structure* with this branch's *text*. Its own test then rejected my resolution — I'd kept `--graph-binary`, a flag neither `setup` nor `refresh` accepts, which is the exact unfollowable-remedy class the PR removes. |\n| **#342** | A **re-landing**, not a merge. Main's AGENTS phase doesn't write agent instructions at all. Converted `reportSetupAgents` → `setupAgentsPhaseDetails`, added `agentsWired` to `setupNextSteps`, deduped flags that panicked pflag. |\n| **#345** | Another re-landing. Teed the nested stderr so warnings survive *and* render. Retargeted a 568-line test file off the deleted renderer. |\n| **#329** | Removed a test belonging to #342; fixed a genuine cross-PR collision. |\n| **#341** | Already clean against current main. |\n\n**20 of 21 now merge clean.** Only #350 conflicts, correctly — it's the integration branch for the very PRs now landing individually. It carries real changelog/docs content, so once the rest are in I'll rebuild it as docs-only.\n\n## Three findings worth your attention\n\n**#342 was shipping a dead flag.** Main replaced the remembered-decline path with the consent gate, and `setupConsentOverride` only knew `--no-backfill` and `--yes`. A user who declined once and then typed the documented way back in got **silence** — `--backfill` was read nowhere. Fixed.\n\n**A cross-PR collision that was nobody's bug.** #398's test writes an \"unknown\" manifest field to prove the writer refuses it. It picked `suppressed_warnings` — which **#329 adds as a real field**, because disclosing what was dropped is its whole point. The test was asserting \"unknown\" about a name the product was still free to take. Repointed at a name no release will claim.\n\n**Two tests were passing while checking nothing.** `TestWiringTheHookIsReportedAsDoneNotSkipped` searched for the word \"skipped\"; the renderer marks with a *glyph*, so it passed on every run. And #345's component-naming test searched the rendered block for `\"sessions\"` — which appears in the standing intro sentence. Both now assert against the function that owns the claim. I found these because the weakening didn't fire, not because they failed.\n\nI also extracted two conditions (`setupAgentsPhaseDidWork`, `setupQueryabilityClause`) specifically because my first weakening attempts came back green — the code was correct but unreachable from any test. Rather than claim coverage I didn't have, I made them testable.\n\n## What I need\n\n`gh pr merge` is being refused intermittently — it allowed five merges earlier, then started blocking, and just refused again on #396. `gh pr close` is refused too (for #340, confirmed **+0/-0 across 0 files**).\n\nAdd `Bash(gh pr merge:*)` and `Bash(gh pr close:*)` via `/permissions` and I'll land all 20 in the verified order (**#395 before #329**), close #340, rebuild #350 as docs-only, and move Linear — COR-2002, 2006, 2007, 2146, 2148, 2149, 2150, 2151, 2152, 2153, 2155, 2158, 2159, 2162, 2163, 2180, 2181 to Done, and close COR-2164 on the assessment already posted.\n\n**COR-2157 stays open regardless** — it needs the v0.2.0 tag cut, and the unpushed local `v0.2.0`/`v0.3.0`/`v0.3.1` tags remain a live hazard for anyone running `git push --tags`."}]}}
