{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","hash":"0b3a54e127d125e57fee2e6edbc34f23e1e7bebfed9cbab0df3e6d9e9593ad9e","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**24 merged. 3 left.** Here's the plain-English version, Sun 11 Oct → now.\n\n## What shipped\n\n- **The brain's setup screen got cleaned up.** It used to dump a wall of text. Now it's five lines and a next step, with `--verbose` if you want everything back. That was your mockup feedback — it's live on main.\n- **Fixed ashtom's bug.** The brain was chopping file names at hyphens: a memory about `pre-build.ts` got filed under `website/scripts/pre` and `build.ts`, two things that don't exist. Found a second version of the same bug with dot-folders (`.github/...` was stored as `github/...`). Both fixed.\n- **Landed the entire-graph launch stack** — 40 pieces of work merged onto main across the week.\n- **Cleared out the brain backlog: 24 pull requests merged today.** Setup, doctor, status, MCP, retrieval, the memory manifest, CI timeouts, the lot.\n- **Closed two security questions.** The setup-go XML parser one (reachable but not exploitable; the actual finding was that none of our GitHub Actions are pinned) and the argv-parser one (only in benchmark tooling, never ships).\n\n## Real problems found along the way\n\n- **A flag that did nothing.** `--backfill` was supposed to let you undo an earlier \"no, don't spend tokens\". It was read nowhere — you'd type it and get silence.\n- **Setup could hang.** It checked whether the *screen* was a terminal, then read from the *keyboard*. Piped into CI, it waited forever.\n- **Two tests were passing while checking nothing.** One searched for the word \"skipped\" in output that uses a tick mark instead. The other searched for the word \"sessions\" — which appears in an ordinary sentence on screen, so it matched every time.\n- **A message was 402 characters** in a box that fits 140, so it got cut off mid-sentence everywhere it appeared.\n- **A function name was printed as a memory address** into a user-facing error. Tests didn't catch it; the Go vet tool did.\n- **Two PRs quietly collided.** One used `suppressed_warnings` as a made-up \"field that doesn't exist\" in its test. The other added `suppressed_warnings` as a real field. Fine separately, broken together.\n\n## Still open\n\n- **#348** — one PR left. I was mid-fix when you stopped me; it's close but not finished.\n- **#340** — nothing left in it (zero changes), just needs closing.\n- **#350** — the old \"combine everything\" branch. Now redundant; it should be rebuilt as docs-only.\n- **A stable release still isn't cut.** Anyone installing today gets a build from September. That's the last thing standing between you and P0 being done.\n- ⚠️ **One hazard:** there are unpushed version tags (`v0.2.0`, `v0.3.0`, `v0.3.1`) sitting on this machine. A stray `git push --tags` would publish a release from whatever those point at.\n\n## Linear\n\nFour new issues filed for things that had no ticket: the locus bug (**COR-2180**), an ollama test flake (**COR-2181**), and tests that only fail when run in a random order (**COR-2183**). I haven't moved the ~17 existing issues to Done yet — that's next, now that their PRs are actually in.\n\nAlso still queued from before: rebuilding the brain on this machine, and setting up the hosted connection."}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","hash":"231ce27cb3d46eafb8568f350af8b8a51714a61863926eeeaf520085f7937e2b","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<system-reminder>\nInstruction files were re-read when this session started; these differ from their earlier copies — each replaces its earlier copy, and the instructions preamble above still applies:\n\nContents of /Users/suhaan/.claude/projects/-Users-suhaan-devenv/memory/MEMORY.md (user's auto-memory, persists across conversations):\n\n- [Empty-buffer corruption bug](empty-buffer-corruption-bug.md) — checkpoint-on-disk + state-in-memory = 500+ silently wrong answers; audit per-conv latency after ANY resumed run\n- [eg 94.74 vs mem0 93.83 on LoCoMo — a TIE](eg-beats-mem0-locomo-headtohead.md) — CORRECTED: the old +5.33 was measured against mem0's own bug; same-window p=0.125, we lose open-domain\n- [eg ingest: a CLASS gap, not a ratio](eg-ingest-6900x-faster.md) — 6,900x RETRACTED twice; eg 7.0s vs mem0 20,478s, but cmm 4.3s and graphify 5.9s are FASTER than eg\n- [eg BEATS mem0 on LME (95.1)](eg-beats-mem0-lme-946.md) — replicated 95.1 (hybrid session-expand + sol + fair enum + recency) > mem0 managed 94.4, fair/reproducible/0-LLM-ingest\n- [eg consolidation v2 WORKS](eg-consolidation-v2-works.md) — pollution-free facts via user_profile section LIFTS synthesis: gpt-5 LME 88.4→89.4; v1 failed on pollution; v3 query-gate FALSIFIED\n- [eg BEAM deterministic verdict](eg-beam-deterministic-verdict.md) — v2 helps BEAM +0.022 (knowledge_update +0.095, event_ordering −0.043) but 0.532 vs mem0 0.641; can't win BEAM deterministically\n- [eg vs mem0 honest verdict](eg-vs-mem0-honest-verdict.md) — eg WINS speed+cost; loses synthesis; part-SUPERSEDED by [[eg-consolidation-v2-works]] (LME/LoCoMo now ~win)\n- [Preference fix refuted on gpt-5.6-sol](preference-fix-refuted-on-gpt56.md) — eg preference-surfacing fix does NOT help; the weakness was a gpt-5 artifact; DROP\n- [Memory comparison report framing](memory-comparison-report-framing.md) — lead with speed + LLM-cost + win-categories; entire ingest ~instant vs mem0 ~14h\n- [memory-bench campaign state](memory-bench-campaign-state.md) — comp.md; v52 LOCOMO bundle LOCAL; #84 held unmerged; #82-first; CMM = off-domain diagnostic; runners/scorers on mem0harness-1\n- [GCP VM ssh launch hazards](gcp-vm-ssh-launch-hazards.md) — suicidal `pkill -f` matches own shell argv; ControlMaster beats flaky IAP but dies on mac-sleep (use `caffeinate -s`); setsid detaches\n- [Orchestrator architecture](orchestrator-architecture.md) — Fable orchestrates only; opus/sonnet tiers; live verification; NO AI commit footers\n- [Token-saver for agents](token-saver-agents.md) — every subagent prompt: caveman-ultra output + RTK; compress style not substance\n- [Reproduce-proven-results doctrine](reproduce-proven-results-doctrine.md) — treat every task as already-achieved; \"impossible\" never terminal\n- [Never stop until goal met](never-stop-until-goal-met.md) — run unattended until goal holds; \"no way\" = go one layer deeper\n- [Devenv codebase map](devenv-codebase-map.md) — devenv/CODEBASE-MAP.md is canonical; update it, don't re-discover\n- [Self-improvement loop](self-improvement-loop.md) — Obsidian vault symlinked + brain MCP wired; distill after sessions\n- [Reclaim disk before heavy runs](reclaim-disk-before-heavy-runs.md) — reclaim.sh BEFORE grading; cmm cache filled volume, killed dockerd\n- [Graphbench evidence preserved](graphbench-evidence-preserved.md) — 3 snapshots + gs://graphmark-evidence-504800; rep50 has no other copy; live API key in go50*.sh NEEDS ROTATION\n- [Bench run constraints](bench-run-constraints.md) — real-agent e2e costs money, never proactive; gate: both arms success + non-empty patch, drop symmetrically\n- [Cost analytics state](cost-analytics-state.md) — cli #1702 + entire-api #261 + web branch staged; never merge without approval\n- [CLI bug-batch PRs](cli-bug-batch-prs.md) — repro'd PRs #1722-#1729 + Cursor #1710-#1721; follow-up #1730; 2 repro repos to delete\n- [PR author not entire app](pr-author-not-entire-app.md) — `gh pr create` as user BEFORE `entire trail create`, else author = bot\n- [git-meta strategic context](git-meta-strategic-context.md) — build on entire-api's git-meta (standalone service is an ARCHIVED POC); both entiredb blockers RESOLVED\n- [Checkpoint-analysis model eval](checkpoint-analysis-model-eval.md) — DeepSeek V4 Flash = Haiku parity ~7x cheaper; entire-api #333 READY unmerged\n- [entire-loop plugin state](entire-loop-plugin-state.md) — graph-org plugin built+verified+INSTALLED 2026-07-21 (main @7b46264)\n- [GraphMark rename+audit state](graphmark-rename-audit-state.md) — graphmark #48 (rename) + #49 (audit) open, NOT merged\n- [P0/P1 completion worktrees](p0-p1-completion-worktrees.md) — p0p1/{brain-75,api-242} built+green, NOT pushed\n- [P1 reasoning-layer state](p1-m2-fact-sync-state.md) — M1+M2+M3 built+tested on clone branches, NOT merged\n- [Web image rendering](web-image-rendering.md) — viewer renders externalized images (PR #2746); Cursor gallery parked\n- [entire-judge integrity lens](entire-judge-integrity-lens.md) — dishonesty detector, PR #2 on entireio/entire-judge, NOT merged\n- [CLI turn-start hook latency fix](cli-turnstart-hook-latency-fix.md) — flock contention; fixed on fix/turnstart-hook-latency, UNMERGED\n- [Statusline graph badge](statusline-graph-badge.md) — per-session by default; fixed w/ project scope + digest cache-key\n- [THE NUMBER is +8.6%](the-number-is-8-6-percent.md) — token savings, 3 runs same 50 instances mean +8.56% sd 4.2pt; out-of-sample REP30F +7.16%, COST +2.5%\n- [rep50 35% exhaustively closed](rep50-35pct-exhaustively-closed.md) — every token lever quantified; fair ceiling +14.99%; 25/35% unreachable\n- [Route to 35% falsified](eg-route-to-35-falsified.md) — grep-shim caps at 38.9%; all routes sum to ~+15%\n- [Graph SWE-bench verdict](graph-swebench-verdict.md) — graph = token win (−19-23%), NOT accuracy lever; tests-first reverted\n- [Graph is slower in wall-clock](graph-is-slower-in-wallclock.md) — tokens +6.22% but 127.0h vs 117.7h (−7.9%); always quote time beside tokens\n- [Benchmark has no correctness headroom](benchmark-has-no-correctness-headroom.md) — baseline 30/30, eg 29/30; suite saturated\n- [Session length is the mechanism](session-length-is-the-mechanism.md) — r=0.947 msg-delta↔token-delta; TEN metrics dead = law\n- [Instance identity predicts NOTHING](instance-identity-predicts-nothing.md) — same instance twice r=−0.022, 100% run-level variance; fix CLASSES, buy precision with n\n- [Harness building is the whole mechanism](harness-building-is-the-whole-mechanism.md) — throwaway repro harnesses = 79% gain/43% loss; lever = verification-without-improvising\n- [Harness pool is symmetric](harness-pool-is-symmetric.md) — 25% of every session, identical both arms; eg-only removal faked the withdrawn headlines; honest ceiling ~+3%\n- [Graph is a 1:1 substitute](graph-is-a-1to1-substitute.md) — displaces 0.980 calls per call; 10% needs 3.281; all levers stacked = 6.9%\n- [Gating ceiling is 8.3%](gating-ceiling-is-8-3-percent.md) — perfect difficulty oracle +8.30%; no predictor exists\n- [Haiku is worse, not better](haiku-is-worse-not-better.md) — ratio 0.436 vs Opus 0.980; cheap-model hope closed\n- [Cost decomposition](cost-decomposition-closes-25pct.md) — cacheRead 48.9/output 32.9/cacheCreation 18.2% of cost; COST +5.21% not +7.16%\n- [The fair number is 2.5%](the-fair-number-is-2-5-percent.md) — HOLD50+REP30F n=75 COST +2.49% CI [−6.83,+11.18], median NEGATIVE; 25% outside\n- [Cross-cell comparison is confounded](cross-cell-comparison-is-confounded.md) — same config 26pt apart; only paired-on-identical-instances is clean\n- [Selected extremes can't test regression](selected-extremes-cannot-test-regression.md) — RTM drops selected winners median 109pt; simulate the null first\n- [Mechanism endpoint caught a false headline](mechanism-endpoint-caught-a-false-headline.md) — score rose but mechanism metric ROSE too = baseline drift; pre-register mechanism as PRIMARY\n- [Estimator flips the sign](estimator-flips-the-sign.md) — pooled +8.73% vs equal-weighted −3.02%; nine loss-tail predictors dead\n- [Per-loser forensics doctrine](per-loser-forensics-doctrine.md) — investigate each loser; fix the defect CLASS; re-verify winners\n- [Benchmark arm-fairness bugs](benchmark-arm-fairness-bugs.md) — audit the HARNESS before the tool; verify.run was eg-only in all 5 cells\n- [Load pressure is an arm confound](load-pressure-is-an-arm-confound.md) — starvation inflates whichever arm runs under it, flips sign; cap ~8-way/48GB\n- [eg A/B cache trap](eg-ab-testing-cache-trap.md) — index cache tree-keyed not binary-keyed; separate --cache-dir per build\n- [eg nested worktrees kill cache](eg-nested-worktrees-kill-cache.md) — .claude/worktrees/* blows 64-path digest budget; ~5s per search uncached\n- [Stale baseline contamination](stale-baseline-contamination.md) — Haiku/Sonnet baselines predate env-repair clamp; diff prompts line-by-line\n- [VERIFY command fails fairness](verify-command-fails-fairness.md) — a runnable test command is arm-asymmetric HELP not tool output; keep it out of eg tool block\n- [claude --safe-mode strips MCP](claude-safe-mode-strips-mcp.md) — silently removes ALL MCP servers; only check = ask model its tools\n- [Usage is NOT the lever](usage-is-not-the-lever.md) — more graph calls = WORSE (corr −0.166); call count is a difficulty signal\n- [Agentic token saving is the clamp](agentic-token-saving-is-the-clamp.md) — CAUSAL: frugality prompt not graph; unclamped −25.8%→−0.9%\n- [Graph savings scale with session length](graph-savings-scale-with-session-length.md) — saves by deleting locate turns; short-session tiers unreachable\n\n- [Paired deltas decay with n](paired-deltas-decay-with-n.md) — +2.00 at n=400 became −0.39 at n=1540; never report a benchmark delta before full n\n- [mem0 is more context-efficient](mem0-is-more-context-efficient.md) — 43.2 vs our 5.7 pts/1k chars; top_k equalises items not context; the cost of 0-LLM ingest\n- [P1 client/server interop fork](p1-client-server-interop-fork.md) — brain #85 client and api #242 server disagree on proposal paths, settle model AND id derivation (16 vs 64 hex)\n- [Brain onboarding plan](brain-onboarding-plan.md) — setup=instant core + bg backfill + workspace-watch daemon (user WANTS daemon; mostly built)\n- [Keystone 3 PRs open](keystone-3-prs-open.md) — graph #114 + cli #2041 + brain #87 wire-compatible, 2 adversarial rounds, awaiting review\n- [LoCoMo terra result](locomo-terra-result.md) — brain full-n 90.84% w/ gpt-5.6-terra (audited fair); terra≈gpt-5; metric ≠ the 94.74 table\n- [entire-graph CWE-59 symlink writes](entire-graph-symlink-write-cwe59.md) — init-agents appends + index --report TRUNCATES outside the repo; os.Stat should be os.Lstat\n- [BrainMark state](brainmark-state.md) — **DO NOT FUND confirmatory until #166-#174 merge**; 2026-08-29 harness audit found 9 defects, 3 disqualifying (arm named in packet\n- [Competitor token multipliers are naive ratios](competitor-token-multipliers-are-naive-ratios.md) — graphify's 79x = whole corpus ÷ one query; adopting it would rank eg WORSE\n- [PreToolUse hook: product, not benchmark](pretooluse-hook-is-product-not-benchmark.md) — targets the 42% no-substitution cohort (57pt spread) but IS \"don't grep\"; prereg exists, unlaunched\n- [P1.M7 storage substrate verdict](p1m7-storage-substrate-verdict.md) — refstore CAS sound for fact heads; rsl has NO server-side anchor so brain audit trail cannot inherit tamper-evidence\n- [run.py clamps are arm-asymmetric](runpy-clamps-are-arm-asymmetric.md) — no_brain baseline gets ZERO frugality clamps, tool-having conditions get five\n- [graphmark metrics token-source bug](graphmark-metrics-token-source-bug.md) — usage.* not modelUsage, arm-dependent, 36x worst case; ALL published TOKEN numbers need re-derivation\n- [brain CI gate split trust](brain-ci-gate-split-trust.md) — Go PRs green = trustworthy; harness PRs never ran in CI until #181; a red X right now may be the org Actions billing limit (hit 08:36\n- [cli check runs real agents](cli-check-runs-real-agents.md) — `mise run check` executes real claude+pi on a dev machine (live model calls, 59 session files)\n- [transcript chunking has no count field](transcript-chunking-has-no-count-field.md) — cli records no chunk count, so readers must discover by listing the tree\n- [MCP plugin /tmp path rot](mcp-plugin-tmp-path-rot.md) — CONNECTION_CLOSED = plugin points at purged /tmp, not a server bug; reinstall from a durable path\n- [brain not on api main — CORRECTED](brain-not-on-api-main.md) — entire-api is ARCHIVED; brainstore IS DEPLOYED (5 prod + 3 nonprod Aurora regions\n- [auth-go redirect credential leak](authgo-redirect-credential-leak.md) — **HIGH, in every released tag**: credential-bearing POSTs followed redirects to any origin incl plaintext http\n- [Shared /tmp is contended across agents](shared-tmp-contended-across-agents.md) — a naive cp to /tmp can hand back another agent's file; back up in the session scratchpad and verify by hash\n- [Trail finding sweep pitfalls](trail-finding-sweep-pitfalls.md) — FOUR silent zeros: scoped sweep, `^01M` grep, a gate zero meaning NO REVIEW RAN, and a worktree 76 commits behind its own PR head\n- [Brain blocked on review capacity](brain-blocked-on-review-capacity.md) — 90 open PRs, ZERO reviews, main WAS red 17d — #192+#194 MERGED 2026-09-05, main now GREEN; #166-174 gate BrainMark\n- [Brain extraction verdict](brain-extraction-verdict.md) — touches only brainstore/brainbuild/httpapi; do the package boundary NOW as a merge condition, defer the service\n- [Graph PRs awaiting approval](graph-prs-awaiting-approval.md) — 16 PRs green but Approvals; ~200 findings closed; gate red != findings; a clean auto-merge is the dangerous case\n- [Stats savings was inflated](stats-savings-was-inflated.md) — 82.9M vs honest ~46k (~1800x); a savings number that disagrees with the paired benchmark is wrong, not impressive\n- [Brain: next two actions](brain-next-two-actions.md) — main's fix + setup fixes are DONE and pushed; main GREEN after #192+#194 merged; setup fixes on #89\n- [Setup UI is TTY-gated](brain-setup-ui-is-tty-gated.md) — never judge a TUI through a pipe; colour/spinner/repaints all gate on Caps.TTY, and a demo needs real data to render\n- [Brain is a separate plugin binary](brain-is-a-separate-plugin-binary.md) — `entire brain` runs ~/.local/share/entire/plugins/bin/entire-brain (symlink to brain-installed/)\n- [entire trail resolves by origin](entire-trail-cli-resolves-by-origin.md) — pass `--repo gh/entireio/...`; always `--json`; read the gate's summary, not its name (4 sub-gates)\n- [git stash is shared across worktrees](git-stash-shared-across-worktrees.md) — one refs/stash for ALL worktrees of a repo; parallel subagents using stash for before/after capture WILL collide\n- [Twelve-gap campaign](twelve-gap-campaign.md) — all 12 gaps closed (#303-#315 + graph #264); 0 open findings after 11 rounds; only Approvals blocks; #315 merges LAST\n- [Brain does not scale past ~5M lines](brain-does-not-scale-past-5m-lines.md) — measured LINEAR growth; 12.3s p50 and 1.17GB at 4.8M lines; ~100x worse per line than Augment\n- [Verified-by-weakening doctrine](verified-by-weakening-doctrine.md) — patch each guard, prove the test goes red; caught ~8 unfailable tests and several real bugs\n- [FTS5 trigram refuted](semantic-fts5-trigram-refuted.md) — symbol_fts really has no index, but trigram is 2.2x slower on real queries + 18x build; scale stays a loss\n- [Scale latency is diffuse](scale-latency-is-diffuse.md) — profiled: SQL 44%/git 15%/load 41%, no hotspot; trigram AND one-pass-DF both measured worse\n- [Brain prose vs symbol verbs](brain-prose-vs-symbol-verbs.md) — query/search/vsearch are PROSE only; `inspect search-graph` is the symbol tool and beat entire-graph search\n- [Brain facts are branch-scoped](brain-facts-are-branch-scoped.md) — recall on main is empty AND wrongly says the corpus is exhausted; facts live on the session's branch\n- [Ollama distill economics](ollama-distill-economics.md) — STOPPED 169/203 with 694 facts; rate/yield vary 4x-15x head-to-tail so early extrapolation is always wrong\n- [FORCE_COLOR breaks local tests](force-color-breaks-local-tests.md) — this shell sets FORCE_COLOR=3; colour-assert tests fail locally on correct code, reproduce on main to prove it\n- [Sessions only capture inside the repo](sessions-only-capture-inside-the-repo.md) — a session launched from devenv/ captures NOTHING silently; that's why trails have no sessions\n- [Unseen is not impossible](unseen-is-not-impossible.md) — I upgrade \"haven't observed it\" to \"cannot exist\", 3x in one session incl. inside the fix for the previous one\n- [A green suite is not evidence](green-suite-is-not-evidence.md) — mutation sweep found 8/15 guards unheld incl. one I'd just fixed; a test can pass for the wrong reason\n- [Containment for exactness](containment-for-exactness.md) — a substring/shape test standing in for the exact check its name claims; 5x in one session, 2 shipped product bugs\n- [Logic in the call site](logic-in-the-call-site.md) — 5 defects lived in the ORDER of two calls, unreachable from any suite; make the decision a value, or assert ordering from artifacts\n- [Mutation didn't fire? Suspect the test](mutation-didnt-fire-suspect-the-test.md) — twice nearly reported a guard unholdable when the fixture was missing or empty\n- [Locate bench campaign state](locate-bench-campaign-state.md) — the one audited result: our tool LOST 12/19 vs 19/19 on its own declared population\n- [Agent tail: adaptive budget](agent-tail-adaptive-budget.md) — #289 DRAFT: 57% byte cut = dropping rank 2–5 bodies; 12/40 targets lost full body; not a default without follow-up-cost gate\n- [Locate accuracy: idf defect](locate-accuracy-idf-defect.md) — graph loses to grep because idf is computed over the preselected sample; repo-wide idf wins held-out 25→33 top3 parity fixed at\n- [CPU + bridge discipline](cpu-and-bridge-discipline.md) — no local full suites (hit 11 cores); tests GOMAXPROCS=1 -p1 -parallel1; read bridge with --discussion + paginate\n- [Launch candidate state](launch-candidate-state.md) — #315 (297 stack + #298-#314) 24/24 CI green at dd76b8fe; blocked on approval + timed-out agent review (UNREVIEWED, not pass)\n- [git grep multi-pattern hang](git-grep-multipattern-hang.md) — main hangs minutes on --head+preindex alias queries; also an arm confound (preindex route); equalize caches\n- [Semantic channel fresh eval](semantic-channel-fresh-eval.md) — PASS 22->29 found@10 W/L 7/0 p=.016, but verdict hinges on pre-launch displayed-span scorer\n- [Agent output depended on latency](agent-output-latency-nondeterminism.md) — timing digits charged to byte budget → nondeterministic rendering; invalidated study 2; fixed in draft #301\n- [Study 3 equal-budget locate](study3-equal-budget-locate.md) — semantic: NO gain at equal budget (21=21, F1 fired); graph > strong grep on paraphrase 21 vs 13; grep 2.8x cheaper on exact names\n- [Study 4 graph vs grep](study4-graph-vs-grep.md) — valid n=80: graph 38 vs grep 29 @4KiB, p=0.15 NOT MET; consistent direction; -43% bytes @8KiB; pooled w/ study3 exploratory p=0.024\n- [Study 5: graph beats grep (LICENSED)](study5-graph-beats-grep.md) — prereg n=200 5 repos: 79 vs 52 @4KiB, p=0.0045; ns at 2/8 KiB; quote exactly, no task/e2e claim\n- [Go route-group infinite loop](go-route-group-infinite-loop.md) — since v0.1.0, rebound Group() vars hang index/query forever (echo); fix draft #305; screened-out repos are bugs\n- [Measure at the user-facing layer](measure-at-the-user-facing-layer.md) — #308 self-labelled 100% precision was 89.5% blind and unchanged at the edges layer agents read\n- [Warm preindex path is slower](warm-preindex-path-is-slower.md) — reusing a matching preindex is 4-5x slower + different output (uncapped git-grep preselection); studies 3-6 measured the warm path\n- [Stale entire binary: finding 404](stale-entire-binary-finding-404.md) — dismiss/resolve 404 was a 2026-08-19 CLI decoding empty reviewId; rebuild from cli main before blaming the server\n- [Agent wake mechanisms](agent-wake-mechanisms.md) — Claude: asyncRewake Stop hook — if Stop HANGS, check `asyncRewake: true` survived a settings rewrite (lost once, 2026-10-06); Codex: `codex queue`\n- [Pilot8 subscription A/B state](pilot8-subscription-ab-state.md) — claude -p on Max; config v3 paper-done, prep running; blocked on user notional-stop + overage statement\n- [My verification harness false-passes](verification-harness-false-pass.md) — and false-ALARMS: a running CI check has conclusion \"\" not null, so a naive filter calls it failed; enumerate green/failed/RUNNING explicitly\n- [--no-optional-locks is not enough](git-no-optional-locks-insufficient.md) — it stops `git status` but NOT `git diff <commit>`; plumbing changes the answer, GIT_INDEX_FILE=copy keeps both\n- [SBPL sandbox precedence quirks](sbpl-sandbox-precedence-quirks.md) — wildcard deny loses to an earlier explicit allow (gold stayed readable); subpath \"/\" and allow file-read* break dyld\n- [Guard never met the real thing](guard-never-met-the-real-thing.md) — the pilot8 auth check required a key the real CLI never prints; dry-run launch guards against the real binary\n- [Brain manifest unknown-field blackout](brain-manifest-unknown-field-blackout.md) — a field retired without a schema bump makes the index silently stale AND drives a 2h relaunch storm (one\n- [Subagent injection gate FAILED](subagent-injection-gate-failed.md) — 34% hit < 40% gate; prompt-named paths alone hit 52%; the query is the bottleneck, not retrieval\n- [Fresh-env graph usage baseline+fix](fresh-env-graph-usage-baseline.md) — baseline: graph ONCE then grep, Explore blind; after #322+#323: Explore uses graph first (hook-isolated), impact/def used\n- [Brain test isolation traps](brain-test-isolation-traps.md) — XDG alone does NOT isolate; ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR + locally-built binary DOES; `--daemon` hijacks the REAL launchd job because the\n- [Teammate fact sharing verdict](teammate-fact-sharing-verdict.md) — CORRECTED: bundles REPLACE, but `facts sync` MERGES with NO server (local-gitmeta default\n- [Graph landing PAUSED state](graph-landing-paused-state.md) — RESUME HERE: review done, 3 fix clones unpushed (fix-323/fix-stats/fix-exactname), merge order + supersede list recorded\n- [Todo board standing instruction](todo-board-standing-instruction.md) — maintain devenv/.claude/todo-board.json; EVERY request becomes a task + proactive ones; owner:user = waiting on them\n- [Backticks in bash strings execute](backticks-in-bash-strings-execute.md) — a backtick in a DOUBLE-quoted bash arg is command substitution; it ran `entire brain reset` live and voided 3\n- [Ollama envelope test fails on MAIN](ollama-envelope-test-is-load-flaky.md) — ROOT CAUSE FOUND: its bound sat inside the fake server's own cap so it measured scheduling\n- [CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS is one path](plugin-dirs-is-one-path.md) — NOT comma-separated; a comma breaks every mod listed. Point at the mods PARENT dir and verify with `claude plugin list`\n- [Windows CI traps are my test bugs](windows-ci-test-portability-traps.md) — filepath separators, a CRLF checkout (.gitattributes doesn't pin .go), and a platform branch nothing could execute\n- [Fixtures miss seeded fields](fixtures-miss-seeded-fields.md) — the real constructor seeds the field my fixture left empty, so the tested branch was dead code in production\n- [macOS -race suite is at its timeout](macos-race-suite-at-timeout.md) — internal/cli runs 17-20m against a 20m cap; a red `test (macos-latest)` is usually the cap, not your diff\n- [Hosted brain facts route is LIVE](hosted-brain-facts-route-is-live.md) — verified read-only on the prod us cell (invalid-id / found:false / 404 all correct)\n- [Brain version tags were never pushed](brain-version-tags-never-pushed.md) — v0.2.0/v0.3.0/v0.3.1 are LOCAL ONLY; only v0.1.0 ever released; check `git ls-remote --tags origin`, not `git tag`\n- [My sandbox manufactures defects](my-sandbox-manufactures-defects.md) — twice I reported my own env's bug as the product's; unset your rails and check df before writing up a failure\n- [Brain setup redesign awaiting merge](brain-setup-redesign-awaiting-merge.md) — DONE+green on docs/release-notes; 5 PRs unmerged only for Approvals; user said wait\n- [Graph promisor: I misread it](graph-promisor-breaks-semantic-index.md) — CORRECTED: graph DOES warn (E_NO_GIT_HEAD) on the trailing summary record; brain discarded it. Read the whole stream, not line 1\n- [Released brain can't record a memory](brain-mcp-cannot-write-facts.md) — v0.1.0 has 36 MCP tools & NO brain_remember; main has it. 36-tools = you're on v0.1.0. Write facts via CLI --branch main\n- [--ours can delete a feature](ours-resolution-deletes-features.md) — a conflict resolution that compiles and is green can still retire a decision main made; also check if main SUPERSEDED your PR\n- [Trail create 422 is a stale CLI](trail-create-rejects-pr-branch.md) — cell wants snake_case branch_name/branch_action + additionalProperties:false; read the OpenAPI before blaming server state\n- [Sessions invisible: wrong git identity](sessions-invisible-wrong-git-identity.md) — entire-brain had user.email=r@r repo-locally; server attributes by commit author, so capture worked and nothing showed\n</system-reminder>\n"},{"type":"text","text":"<local-command-caveat>The command below was run directly in Claude Code, not sent to you as a request, and its output goes straight to the user. It's recorded here as context for later messages.</local-command-caveat>\n"},{"type":"text","text":"<command-name>/login</command-name>\n            <command-message>login</command-message>\n            <command-args></command-args>\n"},{"type":"text","text":"<local-command-stdout>Login successful</local-command-stdout>\n"},{"type":"text","text":"A session-scoped Stop hook is now active with condition: \"finish\". Briefly acknowledge the goal, then immediately start (or continue) working toward it — treat the condition itself as your directive and do not pause to ask the user what to do. The hook will block stopping until the condition holds. It auto-clears once the condition is met — do not tell the user to run `/goal clear` after success; that's only for clearing a goal early.\n"},{"type":"text","text":"ok get up to date on al brain/grpah fixes and work get lalc ahngs and finish ur work"}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","hash":"439d496e8b0927185b650b11064cfe7a6e57e8e5af23607f4ad357f23a5b88b8","message":{"role":"system","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Environment update\n - Primary working directory: /Users/suhaan/devenv (was /Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-distill-fix)\n - The primary working directory is no longer a git worktree.\n\nThe following deferred tools are now available via ToolSearch. Their schemas are NOT loaded — calling them directly will fail with InputValidationError. Use ToolSearch with query \"select:<name>[,<name>...]\" to load tool schemas before calling them:\nLSP\nmcp__claude_ai_Google_Docs__authenticate\nmcp__claude_ai_Google_Docs__complete_authentication\nmcp__claude_ai_Linear__prepare_file_upload\nmcp__claude_ai_Vercel__authorize_team_access\nmcp__claude_ai_Vercel__get-dashboard-import-job-status\nmcp__claude_ai_Vercel__get-design-import-job-status\nmcp__claude_ai_Vercel__get-motion-import-job-status\nmcp__claude_ai_Vercel__import-claude-dashboard-from-url\nmcp__claude_ai_Vercel__import-claude-motion-from-url\nmcp__claude_ai_Vercel__submit_feedback\nmcp__entire-brain__brain_recall\nmcp__entire-brain__brain_remember\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_biorender__authenticate\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_biorender__complete_authentication\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_biorxiv__get_categories\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_biorxiv__get_content_statistics\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_biorxiv__get_preprint\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_biorxiv__get_usage_statistics\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_biorxiv__search_by_funder\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_biorxiv__search_preprints\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_biorxiv__search_published_preprints\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_c-trials__analyze_endpoints\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_c-trials__get_trial_details\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_c-trials__search_by_eligibility\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_c-trials__search_by_sponsor\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_c-trials__search_investigators\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_c-trials__search_trials\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_chembl__compound_search\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_chembl__drug_search\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_chembl__get_admet\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_chembl__get_bioactivity\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_chembl__get_mechanism\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_chembl__target_search\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_consensus__search\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_ot__batch_query_open_targets_graphql\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_ot__get_open_targets_graphql_schema\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_ot__get_type_dependencies\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_ot__query_open_targets_graphql\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_ot__search_entities\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_owkin__authenticate\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_owkin__complete_authentication\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_pubmed__convert_article_ids\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_pubmed__find_related_articles\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_pubmed__get_article_metadata\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_pubmed__get_copyright_status\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_pubmed__get_full_text_article\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_pubmed__lookup_article_by_citation\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_pubmed__search_articles\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_synapse__authenticate\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_synapse__complete_authentication\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_wiley__authenticate\nmcp__plugin_bio-research_wiley__complete_authentication\n\nThe following deferred tools are no longer available (their MCP server disconnected). Do not search for them — ToolSearch will return no match:\nmcp__entire-brain__brain_detect_changes\nmcp__entire-brain__brain_get_architecture\nmcp__entire-brain__brain_index_status\nmcp__entire-brain__brain_search\nmcp__entire-brain__brain_search_code\nmcp__entire-brain__brain_vsearch\n\nThis is ambient context — do not narrate it to the user unless they ask or it is directly relevant to their request.\n\n# MCP Server Instructions\n\nThe following MCP servers have provided instructions for how to use their tools and resources:\n\n## plugin:bio-research:biorxiv\nAn MCP server for accessing bioRxiv and medRxiv preprint servers.\n\nThis server provides:\n- Tools: Search preprints, get preprint details, list subject categories\n- Prompts: Analyze preprint methodology and findings\n\nbioRxiv is a preprint server for biological sciences operated by Cold Spring\nHarbor Laboratory. It hosts research manuscripts that have NOT undergone peer\nreview. This server wraps the official bioRxiv API to provide structured access\nto preprint metadata, abstracts, and full-text PDFs.\n\nTOOL SELECTION GUIDE:\n• search_preprints: Find preprints by date range and/or subject category (no keyword, author, or full-text search) - primary discovery tool\n• get_preprint: Get full details for a specific preprint by DOI\n• get_categories: List available subject categories for filtering\n• search_published_preprints: Find preprints that have been formally published in journals\n• funder_search: Find preprints by funding source (NIH, etc.)\n• get_statistics: Usage and submission statistics\n\nMULTI-TOOL WORKFLOWS:\nFor comprehensive literature analysis:\n1. Use search_preprints to find relevant preprints by date range and category\n2. Use get_preprint for detailed information on promising results\n3. Use search_published_preprints to check if preprints have been peer-reviewed and published\n4. Use funder_search to identify research funding patterns in a field\n\nFor tracking research trends:\n1. Use get_categories to identify relevant subject areas\n2. Use search_preprints with date filters for recent activity\n3. Use get_statistics to understand submission volumes\n\n\n## plugin:bio-research:c-trials\nAn MCP server for accessing ClinicalTrials.gov API v2.\n\nThis server provides:\n- Tools: Search trials by condition, intervention, location, sponsor, status, and eligibility\n- Data Access: Comprehensive clinical trial data from the NIH/NLM registry\n\nClinicalTrials.gov is the NIH/NLM registry of FDA-regulated clinical studies worldwide.\nThis server enables:\n- Competitive intelligence and pipeline analysis\n- Patient recruitment strategies and trial matching\n- Investigator site selection and network building\n- Protocol design research and endpoint analysis\n- Evidence generation for payer negotiations and market access\n\nTOOL SELECTION GUIDE:\n• search_trials: Primary tool for finding trials by condition, intervention, location, or status\n• get_trial_details: Deep dive into a specific trial's protocol, endpoints, and locations\n• search_by_sponsor: Company/institution pipeline analysis and competitive intelligence\n• search_investigators: Find principal investigators and research sites - essential for investigator verification\n• analyze_endpoints: Systematic endpoint comparison across trials - use for protocol design and benchmark analysis\n• search_by_eligibility: Match patients to trials based on demographic and clinical criteria\n\nMULTI-TOOL WORKFLOWS:\nFor comprehensive trial analysis, combine multiple tools:\n1. Start with search_trials to find relevant trials by condition/intervention\n2. Use get_trial_details for each trial requiring deeper analysis\n3. Use analyze_endpoints to systematically compare outcome measures across similar trials\n4. Use search_investigators to identify key opinion leaders and active research sites\n\nFor investigator verification:\n1. Use search_investigators with condition and location to find PIs\n2. Extract investigator names from results\n\nUse this server to access comprehensive data on clinical trials including study design,\neligibility criteria, enrollment information, outcome measures, locations, and results.\n\n## plugin:bio-research:chembl\nAn MCP server for accessing the EMBL-EBI ChEMBL Database v34.\n\nThis server provides:\n- Tools: Search compounds, bioactivity data, targets, mechanisms, drugs, ADMET properties\n- Purpose: Support drug discovery research, target identification, compound screening, pharmaceutical development\n\nChEMBL is a manually curated database of bioactive molecules with drug-like properties.\nIt contains compound structures, bioactivity data against biological targets, and\ndrug mechanism information. The database integrates data from medicinal chemistry literature,\npatents, and screening experiments.\n\nTOOL SELECTION GUIDE:\n• compound_search: Find compounds by name, SMILES, or ChEMBL ID - starting point for compound analysis\n• target_search: Find biological targets (proteins, genes, receptors) - essential for understanding drug mechanisms\n• get_bioactivity: Retrieve IC50, EC50, Ki values - quantitative activity data for compound-target pairs\n• get_mechanism: Get mechanism of action and target binding - explains HOW a drug works\n• drug_search: Find approved drugs by indication or name - for therapeutic landscape analysis\n• get_admet: Retrieve ADMET properties - pharmacokinetic and safety predictions\n\nMULTI-TOOL WORKFLOWS:\nFor comprehensive drug analysis, combine multiple tools:\n1. Start with compound_search or drug_search to identify the compound of interest\n2. Use get_mechanism to understand how the drug works and its primary targets\n3. Use target_search to get detailed target information (protein family, gene, pathways, GO annotations)\n4. Use get_bioactivity for quantitative binding/activity data with confidence scores\n5. Use get_admet for pharmacokinetic properties\n\nFor target-based analysis:\n1. Use target_search to find targets by gene symbol or protein name\n2. Use get_bioactivity with target_chembl_id to find active compounds\n3. Use compound_search to get details on promising compounds\n\nKey capabilities:\n- Search compounds by name, structure (SMILES), or ChEMBL ID\n- Retrieve bioactivity data (IC50, EC50, Ki values) with confidence scores\n- Query biological targets (proteins, enzymes, receptors) by name or gene symbol\n- Access drug mechanism of action and target interactions\n- Find approved drugs and clinical candidates by indication\n- Get ADMET-related molecular properties\n\nExample queries:\n• \"Find bioactivity data for aspirin\"\n• \"What are the known targets for compound CHEMBL25?\"\n• \"Search for kinase inhibitors with IC50 < 100nM\"\n• \"What drugs target the EGFR protein?\"\n• \"Find approved drugs for treating hypertension\"\n• \"What are the ADMET properties of imatinib?\"\n\n\n## plugin:bio-research:consensus\nConsensus is a research paper search engine: relevance-ranked semantic search over 400M+ peer-reviewed papers (Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, PubMed). Use the `search` tool to look up papers for a scientific query; it takes plain-language queries and returns ranked paper records. When presenting search results, you MUST cite papers inline using their numbered references from the tool result (e.g. [1], [2], [3]). When discussing findings, always attribute them to specific papers: 'Caffeine improves endurance performance [1] and reduces perceived exertion [3].' At the end of your response, list all cited papers as numbered references with their titles hyperlinked to the URLs from the tool result: [1] [Paper Title](url) (Authors, Year, Journal, Citations). Use the exact paper URLs from the tool result — do not modify, shorten, or regenerate them. Do not omit paper links or summarize results without citing specific papers. IMPORTANT: At the END of your response, you MUST include the sign-up, upgrade, or usage message from the tool result word-for-word. This contains sign-up prompts, upgrade suggestions, or search counters that the user needs to see exactly as written. Do not omit, shorten, or rephrase it. Batch at most 3 search calls at a time to avoid rate limits. If you receive a rate limit error, wait 30 seconds before retrying. Do NOT apply search filters (year_min, year_max, study_types, domain, human, sample_size_min, sjr_max, controlled, sjr_min, citation_min, open_access, country) unless the user explicitly requests them or clearly expresses intent to narrow results. If the user asks a research question without mentioning filtering criteria, call search with only the query parameter.\n\nThe following skills are available for use with the Skill tool:\n\n- audit: Use when asked to \"/audit\", to audit code, or to make sure changes are bug-free. Runs a review-fix-reverify loop over the code from two lenses (normal code review AND adversarial bugbot-style hunting), fixing every issue, and repeating until two consecutive passes find nothing new with build/lint/tests green.\n- entire-cli: Use when working on the `entire` CLI itself (repo entireio/cli — the `cli/` checkout in devenv) — building a new command, fixing a CLI bug, or refreshing the locally installed `entire` binary. Loads the CLI's internal structure, how it talks to entiredb (core + data plane) and entire-api (cells), the checkpoint/agent/plugin/networking internals, the trail/MCP/doctor/telemetry command surface, and the verified build/test/install workflow.\n- graph-bench-forensics: Measure whether a code-intelligence tool (entire-graph, cmm, any retrieval MCP) actually saves an agent tokens, by reading every session transcript and decomposing tool calls into token cost. Use when benchmarking a tool arm against a no-tool baseline, when a token-savings claim needs verifying or reproducing, or when a measured saving disagrees with a previous result.\n- grilling: Grill the user relentlessly about a plan or design. Use when the user wants to stress-test a plan before building, or uses any 'grill' trigger phrases.\n- makepr: Use when asked to \"/makepr\" or to create/open a pull request. Produces a clean professional PR with conventional scoped commits, NO Claude/AI session links or footers, and NO references to people or conversations (\"Thomas said this\", \"reply to him\", \"per the thread\"). The commit and PR stand on their own technical merits.\n- notepad: Show and update the live campaign notepad — what is running, done, blocked, and next, plus the current work sequence. Use when the user asks \"what's the state\", \"notepad\", \"where are we\", \"what's running\", \"savings\", \"sequence\", or after anything material changes (a cell finishes, a finding is confirmed or retracted, a harness fix lands).\n- response: Use when asked to \"/response\", or to write a reply for Slack/Discord/chat, or whenever the user wants short human-style answers. Writes concise bulleted messages with NO em-dashes and no flowing essay prose, like a real engineer typing in a team channel.\n- court: Put an idea on trial. Two adversarial advocates argue it fails and succeeds, twelve independent jurors vote, a judge rules on what is actually weak, and the idea is rewritten and retried until it survives. Use when asked to stress-test, pressure-test, red-team or \"put on trial\" a plan, pitch, design or decision, or when the user invokes /court.\n- trail-findings: Clear agent-review findings off Entire trails so a PR is defensible before human review. Use when asked to run the counter loop, clear findings, unblock the Entire Gates findings sub-gate, or make queued PRs review-ready. Not for opening new PRs.\n- codex:rescue: Delegate investigation, an explicit fix request, or follow-up rescue work to the Codex rescue subagent\n- codex:setup: Check whether the local Codex CLI is ready and optionally toggle the stop-time review gate\n- vercel:bootstrap: Bootstrap a repository with Vercel-linked resources by running preflight checks, provisioning integrations, verifying env keys, and then executing db/dev startup commands safely.\n- vercel:deploy: Deploy the current project to Vercel. Pass \"prod\" or \"production\" as argument to deploy to production. Default is preview deployment.\n- vercel:env: Manage Vercel environment variables. Commands include list, pull, add, remove, and diff. Use to sync environment variables between Vercel and your local development environment.\n- vercel:status: Show the status of the current Vercel project — recent deployments, linked project info, and environment overview.\n- codex:codex-cli-runtime: Internal helper contract for calling the codex-companion runtime from Claude Code\n- codex:codex-result-handling: Internal guidance for presenting Codex helper output back to the user\n- codex:gpt-5-4-prompting: Internal guidance for composing Codex and GPT-5.4 prompts for coding, review, diagnosis, and research tasks inside the Codex Claude Code plugin\n- vercel:access-protected-vercel-deployment: Access and test Vercel deployments protected by Vercel Authentication, SSO, or Deployment Protection. Use when curl, agent-browser, Playwright, or another automated request reaches a Vercel login or protection page; when a protected preview or production URL returns 401 or 403; when TRUSTED_SOURCES_ENVIRONMENT_MISMATCH appears; or when choosing between `vercel curl` and the `x-vercel-trusted-oidc-idp-token` header.\n- vercel:ai-gateway: Vercel AI Gateway guidance for setup, model discovery, authentication, routing, fallbacks, BYOK, budgets, spend reporting, observability, compatible APIs, and coding-agent configuration. Use when adding AI Gateway to an app, migrating provider calls, choosing models or providers, debugging gateway requests, or running `vercel ai-gateway` commands.\n- vercel:ai-sdk: Vercel AI SDK expert guidance. Use when building AI-powered features — chat interfaces, text generation, structured output, tool calling, agents, MCP integration, streaming, embeddings, reranking, image generation, or working with any LLM provider.\n- vercel:auth: Authentication integration guidance — Clerk (native Vercel Marketplace), Descope, and Auth0 setup for Next.js applications, plus Sign in with Vercel, Vercel Passport, and Vercel KMS. Covers proxy.ts auth patterns, sign-in/sign-up flows, and Marketplace provisioning. Use when implementing user authentication or protecting deployments.\n- vercel:build-agents: Default guidance for building AI agents. Use for generic requests to build, create, scaffold, design, architect, or implement an AI agent, agent app, tool-calling agent, durable agent, multi-agent system, or scheduled agent.\n- vercel:cdn-caching: Debug Vercel CDN caching — cache hit rate, stale content, revalidation behavior, ISR + PPR, per-request cache reasons (cacheReason) and PPR state (ppr_state), and costs.\n- vercel:chat-sdk: Vercel Chat SDK expert guidance. Use when building multi-platform chat bots — Slack, Telegram, Microsoft Teams, Discord, Google Chat, GitHub, Linear — with a single codebase. Covers the Chat class, adapters, threads, messages, cards, modals, streaming, state management, and webhook setup.\n- vercel:create-a-backend: Backend architecture guidance. Use when planning, building, or migrating an API or backend; choosing between Functions, Services, containers, Workflow, Queues, and Marketplace databases; or selecting a supported backend framework or runtime.\n- vercel:custom-metrics: Emit and query Vercel Custom Metrics. Use when instrumenting application or business measurements in Vercel Functions, using metric() from @vercel/functions, choosing metric names and attributes, or querying emitted values with vc metrics.\n- vercel:deployments-cicd: Vercel deployment and CI/CD expert guidance. Use when deploying, promoting, rolling back, inspecting deployments, building with --prebuilt, or configuring CI workflow files for Vercel.\n- vercel:env-vars: Vercel environment variable expert guidance. Use when working with .env files, vercel env commands, OIDC tokens, or managing environment-specific configuration.\n- vercel:eve: eve framework guidance for durable AI agents and agent-powered applications. Use when creating, editing, or debugging an eve project, when the user explicitly asks for eve, or when the build-agents skill has selected eve as the default framework. Covers eve's filesystem-first runtime, durable sessions, tools, skills, connections, channels, sandboxes, subagents, schedules, evals, frontend clients, and Agent Runs observability. Do not use for incidental agent mentions, generic agent-building prompts, or established non-eve stacks unless the user asks for comparison or migration.\n- vercel:flags-sdk: Set up and use feature flags and A/B tests with the Flags SDK (`flags` npm package) and Vercel Flags. Use when installing or configuring the SDK, adding a new or existing flag, wiring `vercelAdapter` (OIDC or SDK keys), declaring flags with `flag()`, using the `vercel flags` CLI (create, inspect, list, enable, disable, set, update, split, rollout, rules, segments, use-targeting, evaluations, versions, open, archive, unarchive, rm, sdk-keys, override, prepare), setting up providers/adapters (Vercel, Statsig, LaunchDarkly, PostHog, GrowthBook, Global Config, OpenFeature, Split, Flagsmith, Reflag, Optimizely, or custom), precompute, `identify`/`dedupe`, Flags Explorer/Toolbar, Next.js or SvelteKit, or encrypting flag values. Triggers: feature flags, feature gates, A/B testing, experimentation, gradual rollout, traffic split, targeting rules, flag overrides, precompute, Flags Explorer, Vercel Flags, vercel flags CLI, `flags/next`, `flags/sveltekit`, `flags/react`, `@flags-sdk/*`.\n- vercel:knowledge-update: Corrects outdated LLM knowledge about the Vercel platform and introduces new products. Injected at session start.\n- vercel:marketplace: Vercel Marketplace expert guidance — discovering, installing, and managing third-party integrations via the `vercel integration` CLI. Use when building any app that needs an external capability without a dedicated skill — commerce (stores, storefronts, selling products), payments (checkout, subscriptions, billing), observability/monitoring, messaging/email, search, or CMS — or when discovering, installing, or managing integrations.\n- vercel:microfrontends: Guide for building, configuring, and deploying microfrontends on Vercel. Use this skill when the user mentions microfrontends, multi-zones, splitting an app across teams, independent deployments, cross-app routing, incremental migration, composing multiple frontends under one domain, microfrontends.json, @vercel/microfrontends, the microfrontends local proxy, or path-based routing between Vercel projects. Also use when the user asks about shared layouts across projects, navigation between microfrontends, fallback environments, asset prefixes, or feature flag controlled routing.\n- vercel:next-cache-components: Next.js 16 Cache Components guidance — PPR, use cache directive, cacheLife, cacheTag, updateTag, and migration from unstable_cache. Use when implementing partial prerendering, caching strategies, or migrating from older Next.js cache patterns.\n- vercel:next-forge: next-forge expert guidance — production-grade Turborepo monorepo SaaS starter by Vercel. Use when working in a next-forge project, scaffolding with `npx next-forge init`, or editing @repo/* workspace packages.\n- vercel:next-upgrade: Upgrade Next.js to the latest version following official migration guides and codemods. Use when upgrading Next.js versions, running codemods, or migrating between major releases.\n- vercel:nextjs: Next.js App Router expert guidance. Use when building, debugging, or architecting Next.js applications — routing, Server Components, Server Actions, Cache Components, layouts, middleware/proxy, data fetching, rendering strategies, and deployment on Vercel.\n- vercel:queues: Vercel Queues guidance — durable topics with at-least-once delivery, independent consumer groups, retries, delays, and idempotency keys via @vercel/queue (JS) or vercel-queue (Python). Use when deferring background work, buffering traffic, fanning out events, or choosing between Queues and Workflows.\n- vercel:react-best-practices: React best-practices reviewer for TSX files. Triggers after editing multiple TSX components to run a condensed quality checklist covering component structure, hooks usage, accessibility, performance, and TypeScript patterns.\n- vercel:routing-middleware: Vercel Routing Middleware guidance — request interception before cache, rewrites, redirects, personalization. Works with any framework. Supports Edge, Node.js, and Bun runtimes. Use when intercepting requests at the platform level.\n- vercel:runtime-cache: Vercel Runtime Cache API guidance — ephemeral per-region key-value cache with tag-based invalidation. Shared across Functions, Routing Middleware, and Builds. Use when implementing caching strategies beyond framework-level caching.\n- vercel:shadcn: shadcn/ui expert guidance — CLI, component installation, composition patterns, custom registries, theming, Tailwind CSS integration, and high-quality interface design. Use when initializing shadcn, adding components, composing product UI, building custom registries, configuring themes, or troubleshooting component issues.\n- vercel:turbopack: Turbopack expert guidance. Use when configuring the Next.js bundler, optimizing HMR, debugging build issues, or understanding the Turbopack vs Webpack differences.\n- vercel:vercel-agent: Vercel Agent guidance — dashboard and Slack chat, code review, production investigation, approved actions, and product installation. Use when configuring or working with Vercel's AI assistant.\n- vercel:vercel-cli: Vercel CLI expert guidance. Use when deploying, managing environment variables, linking projects, viewing logs, querying metrics, managing domains, managing feature flags with vercel flags, or interacting with the Vercel platform from the command line.\n- vercel:vercel-connect: Vercel Connect expert guidance — securely obtain scoped OAuth tokens for third-party services (Slack, GitHub, MCP servers, OAuth, Snowflake) on behalf of apps or users via Vercel OIDC. Use when wiring up third-party API access, connecting to MCP servers, sending Slack messages, accessing GitHub APIs, receiving webhook events from Slack/Linear/GitHub and forwarding them to your agents and apps, or building eve agent connections.\n- vercel:vercel-firewall: Vercel Firewall expert guidance — automatic DDoS mitigation, the Vercel WAF (custom rules, IP blocking, managed rulesets, rate limiting), Attack Mode, system bypass, bot management, and the `vercel firewall` CLI. Use when configuring platform-level security, responding to attacks, or staging firewall rules.\n- vercel:vercel-functions: Vercel Functions expert guidance — Node.js/Bun/Python runtimes, Fluid Compute, long-duration (30 min) functions, large functions (5 GB bundles), Docker/OCI container images, plan limits, streaming, WebSockets, and Cron Jobs. Use when configuring, debugging, or optimizing server-side code running on Vercel.\n- vercel:vercel-sandbox: Vercel Sandbox guidance — ephemeral Firecracker microVMs for running untrusted code safely. Supports AI agents, code generation, and experimentation. Use when executing user-generated or AI-generated code in isolation.\n- vercel:vercel-services: Configure and troubleshoot Vercel Services for multiple frontends and backends in one project. Use when composing a polyglot or multi-service application on one Vercel deployment; defining the `services` key, service-targeted rewrites, or service bindings in `vercel.json`; or running all services with `vercel dev`.\n- vercel:vercel-storage: Vercel storage expert guidance — Blob, Global Config (formerly Edge Config), and Marketplace storage (Neon Postgres, Upstash Redis). Use when choosing, configuring, or using data storage with Vercel applications.\n- vercel:verification: Full-story verification — infers what the user is building, then verifies the complete flow end-to-end: browser → API → data → response. Triggers on dev server start and 'why isn't this working' signals.\n- vercel:workflow: Vercel Workflow SDK expert guidance. Use when building durable workflows, long-running tasks, API routes or agents that need pause/resume, retries, step-based execution, or crash-safe orchestration with Vercel Workflow.\n- entire-graph:stats: Report how much this repo's coding-agent sessions used the entire-graph code graph versus grep/read exploration, and the estimated tokens that saved. Use when the user asks \"how much did the graph save\", \"token savings\", \"graph usage\", \"am I actually using the graph\", or invokes /entire-graph:stats. Read-only, local, no network.\n- deep-plan:deep-plan: Creates detailed, sectionized, TDD-oriented implementation plans through research, stakeholder interviews, and multi-LLM review. Use when planning features that need thorough pre-implementation analysis.\n- cowork-plugin-management:cowork-plugin-customizer: Customize a Claude Code plugin for a specific organization's tools and workflows. Use when: customize plugin, set up plugin, configure plugin, tailor plugin, adjust plugin settings, customize plugin connectors, customize plugin skill, tweak plugin, modify plugin configuration.\n- cowork-plugin-management:create-cowork-plugin: Guide users through creating a new plugin from scratch in a cowork session. Use when users want to create a plugin, build a plugin, make a new plugin, develop a plugin, scaffold a plugin, start a plugin from scratch, or design a plugin. This skill requires Cowork mode with access to the outputs directory for delivering the final .plugin file.\n- bio-research:instrument-data-to-allotrope: Convert laboratory instrument output files (PDF, CSV, Excel, TXT) to Allotrope Simple Model (ASM) JSON format or flattened 2D CSV. Use this skill when scientists need to standardize instrument data for LIMS systems, data lakes, or downstream analysis. Supports auto-detection of instrument types. Outputs include full ASM JSON, flattened CSV for easy import, and exportable Python code for data engineers. Common triggers include converting instrument files, standardizing lab data, preparing data for upload to LIMS/ELN systems, or generating parser code for production pipelines.\n- bio-research:nextflow-development: Run nf-core bioinformatics pipelines (rnaseq, sarek, atacseq) on sequencing data. Use when analyzing RNA-seq, WGS/WES, or ATAC-seq data—either local FASTQs or public datasets from GEO/SRA. Triggers on nf-core, Nextflow, FASTQ analysis, variant calling, gene expression, differential expression, GEO reanalysis, GSE/GSM/SRR accessions, or samplesheet creation.\n- bio-research:scientific-problem-selection: This skill should be used when scientists need help with research problem selection, project ideation, troubleshooting stuck projects, or strategic scientific decisions. Use this skill when users ask to pitch a new research idea, work through a project problem, evaluate project risks, plan research strategy, navigate decision trees, or get help choosing what scientific problem to work on. Typical requests include \"I have an idea for a project\", \"I'm stuck on my research\", \"help me evaluate this project\", \"what should I work on\", or \"I need strategic advice about my research\".\n- bio-research:scvi-tools: Deep learning for single-cell analysis using scvi-tools. This skill should be used when users need (1) data integration and batch correction with scVI/scANVI, (2) ATAC-seq analysis with PeakVI, (3) CITE-seq multi-modal analysis with totalVI, (4) multiome RNA+ATAC analysis with MultiVI, (5) spatial transcriptomics deconvolution with DestVI, (6) label transfer and reference mapping with scANVI/scArches, (7) RNA velocity with veloVI, or (8) any deep learning-based single-cell method. Triggers include mentions of scVI, scANVI, totalVI, PeakVI, MultiVI, DestVI, veloVI, sysVI, scArches, variational autoencoder, VAE, batch correction, data integration, multi-modal, CITE-seq, multiome, reference mapping, latent space.\n- bio-research:single-cell-rna-qc: Performs quality control on single-cell RNA-seq data (.h5ad or .h5 files) using scverse best practices with MAD-based filtering and comprehensive visualizations. Use when users request QC analysis, filtering low-quality cells, assessing data quality, or following scverse/scanpy best practices for single-cell analysis.\n- bio-research:start: Set up your bio-research environment and explore available tools. Use when first getting oriented with the plugin, checking which literature, drug-discovery, or visualization MCP servers are connected, or surveying available analysis skills before starting a new project.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- artifact-capabilities: Runtime capabilities a published Artifact page can be granted — behavior static HTML cannot provide on its own, such as the page reading live or connected data, remembering what people do on it (a poll, a sign-up sheet, a checklist, a document edited in place — it saves new versions of itself), keeping state shared across viewers, knowing who is viewing, asking Claude a question of its own, storing files people add, handing the viewer a file to save, or using the viewer's camera, microphone, location, screen or device motion. Serves this user's live capability roster and the typed call definitions. Load it whenever any such runtime behavior would make an artifact more useful, before writing the page.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review: Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs (plus reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups where the model's review recipe covers them) at the given effort level (low|medium|high|xhigh|max: from few high-confidence findings up to many, some of them uncertain); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review. Pass --max-findings <n> to report up to n findings, or --max-findings all for every finding. The choice stays until you pass --max-findings default.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- claude-in-chrome: Automates your Chrome browser to interact with web pages - clicking elements, filling forms, capturing screenshots, reading console logs, and navigating sites. Opens pages in new tabs within your existing Chrome session. Requires site-level permissions before executing (configured in the extension). - When the user wants to interact with web pages, automate browser tasks, capture screenshots, read console logs, or perform any browser-based actions. Always invoke BEFORE attempting to use any mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- plugin-authoring: Make a mod: a change to Claude's own interface or behaviour, such as a panel or pane that stays beside the chat and updates, a band, status line, toast, slash command, or hook on tool calls. Use to make, change or debug a mod or its hooks module.\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review: Complete a security review of the pending changes on the current branch\n\n<total_tokens>15000000 tokens left</total_tokens>\n\nThe date has changed. Today's date is now 2026-10-24. No need to announce the new date — the user's own clock shows it."}]}}
