head -60; done","description":"Inspect PRs 442, 440, 426"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_01N1sK71S6igCewGb9AUswXW":{"cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-dp2"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"ab13f32b-93de-457f-b396-954745d77c9c","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgMGibFq4EgMqw6QmGUgD","requestRef":"da52f771-805a-4f1f-9767-ba62057d22db","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5[1m]","type":"assistant","uuid":"9216a351-aa3d-458a-ab96-8d8c42f30ff0","timestamp":"2026-10-24T15:07:58.121Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"80327498-7bd2-42a4-985a-9acba8ccaf85","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-dp2","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","version":"2.1.310","gitBranch":"main","slug":"floofy-discovering-bachman"}
{"parentUuid":"9216a351-aa3d-458a-ab96-8d8c42f30ff0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a4a9993a-3afe-49a7-9e77-e184e39740e2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N1sK71S6igCewGb9AUswXW","type":"tool_result","content":"=== PR #442 ===\nauthor: karthik-rameshkumar\ncreated: 2026-10-22T10:41:42Z\nhead: claude/inspiring-wright-68270a -> main\n+428/-11\nfiles: internal/cli/watch.go, internal/cli/watch_test.go\n---BODY---\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.com/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/353\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n## Problem\n\nThe supervised watch daemon (`entire-brain workspace watch --distill`, launchd `io.entire.brain-watch.*`) is the only automatic path that runs fact distillation, and on one machine it lost `locks/write.lock` on **576 of 724 ticks over 20 days**. A failed refresh skips the tick's agent work, so the 24h-gated distill ran **3 times in 20 days**.\n\n## Root cause\n\n| Fact | Value | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| Brain write-lock timeout | 10s | `internal/cli/brain.go:33` (`brainWriteLockTimeout`), shared by ~375 `withBrainWriteLock` callers |\n| Holder named in the timeout message | 12s to 25s old at the moment the waiter gave up (201 at 12s, 150 at 13s, 49 at 14s, ...); 21 ticks saw 1m4s to 15m37s | `~/.local/state/entire/logs/watch.log` |\n| Who holds it | the detached `entire-brain memory worker --once` | `locks/write.lock.owner` and `locks/memory-worker.lock.owner` named the same pid (57068); the worker log shows that pass ran 10:07:23Z to 10:09:45Z |\n| Who launched it | the same tick, one step earlier | `watchTick`: delta → reconcile (`reconcileMemoryAndLaunch` → `memoryWorkerLaunch`) → refresh |\n\nThe worker's prepass (`runRefreshDeltaStats` with export: `runExport` and `buildHistoryShortTermLockedContext`, both under write.lock) and its projection publish hold the lock for 12 to 25 seconds on this brain. The tick's heavy refresh asked for the same lock a second or two after launching that worker, waited 10s in session export (\"session export degraded\"), waited 10s again in seed baseline (\"seed baseline failed\", 652 ticks), and aborted the tick. The delta step, which runs *before* the launch, succeeded on 686 of those ticks, which is the tell that the tick was racing its own child.\n\nWhy every tick: the delta reports \"14 transcripts failed to scan\" on 652 ticks, so every tick is a repair tick (reconcile fires, worker launched, heavy refresh runs), and the repair backoff counter only advances on a successful refresh, so it never backed off while the refresh kept losing.\n\nNot part of this: `dirty_worktree` (91 ticks) is the expected refusal while the tree has uncommitted edits (issue #327) and the tick already continues past it.\n\n## Fix (watch.go only)\n\n1. **Launch the worker after the refresh, not before.** The reconcile step is now a deferred closure with a ran-guard (same shape as `runFactsSync`), called explicitly after the refresh outcome on the main path. It still runs exactly once on every exit path (consolidation deferred, no change, refresh failed).\n2. **Bounded retry on lock contention.** `watchRefreshWithLockRetry` re-attempts the free refresh only when the error is a lock timeout (`errFileLockTimeout` or the `brain_locked:` code), up to 3 retries with 15s / 30s / 60s waits, each capped at `--interval`, cancelled by ctx. 105s of waiting total, inside the 5-minute default interval. A 15s first wait outlasts the remainder of a hold that was already 12 to 25s old when the first attempt timed out. The global 10s timeout is deliberately unchanged: it serves interactive commands too.\n3. **Pending gated work survives a lost lock.** If every attempt loses, and an earlier *successful* refresh (within one `--distill-every` window) left distill or seed pending, the tick runs it with the refresh cursor left where it was, so the next tick retries the refresh. This is the rule the no-change path already applies; it covers the minute-plus holders the retry cannot outwait.\n\n## Tests\n\n`go test ./internal/cli/ -run 'Watch'` is green, including nine new tests in `watch_test.go`: retry after a briefly held lock, bounded give-up with the 15/30/60s schedule, no retry for non-lock failures, ctx cancel during a wait, reconcile ordering on success / failure / deferred paths, pending distill running (and not running past the staleness bound) when the lock keeps being lost, the interval cap, and the error classifier.\n\n## Follow-ups (not in this PR)\n\n- Why 14 transcripts fail to scan on every tick: it turns every tick into a repair tick and launches a worker each time.\n- The worker holds write.lock across its whole short-term build in the prepass. Shortening that hold would cut contention for hooks, the MCP server and `brief` as well.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n=== PR #440 ===\nauthor: karthik-rameshkumar\ncreated: 2026-10-22T06:43:02Z\nhead: fix/distill-cache-salt-agent-independent -> main\n+505/-28\nfiles: internal/cli/distill_cache_salt_test.go, internal/cli/distill_cmd.go, internal/cli/distill_cmd_test.go\n---BODY---\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.com/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/351\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n## Problem\n\nThe distill cache salt hashed the agent, model and effort alongside the prompt, reconcile prompt and threshold. The facts are the product, not the model that produced them, so keying on the model meant the setup backfill (a resolved agent, effort low) and the session-end hook (auto, no effort) never shared a cache entry. Each re-spent on every session the other had already distilled.\n\nOn a real 289-session brain: a bare `entire brain distill --dry-run` reported **0 cached**, while `--agent codex --effort low` reported 72 of the same sessions. No facts had landed from the hook for 16 days.\n\n## Change\n\n- `distillCacheSalt` now covers only what the extractor was asked (prompt with taxonomy, reconcile prompt, confidence threshold) and the preprocessing version.\n- Entries older binaries wrote under the agent-bearing formula are grandfathered against an enumerated set of known producer salts (the current run's own, plus codex and claude-code with and without effort low and a pinned model, in both preprocessing versions) and rewritten under the new salt on first sight. The hook migrates what the backfill wrote and vice versa; nobody has to repeat the writer's flags.\n- The checkpoint fast path, the file-era-to-checkpoint content migration and the per-chunk reuse cache get the same grandfathering.\n- The three salt derivations (current, legacy, legacy agent-bearing) share one prompt/threshold resolution.\n\n## Tests\n\n- `TestDistillCacheSaltIgnoresAgentModelEffort`: salt equality across agent, model, effort and agent command; prompt, reconcile, threshold and preprocessing version still invalidate.\n- `TestDistillGrandfathersAgentSaltedCacheEntries`: seeded legacy entries cost zero agent calls on the backfill run, are rewritten under the new salt, cost zero calls on the hook's configuration, migrate under the hook's configuration too, and changed content still re-distills.\n- `TestLegacyAgentDistillCacheSaltsEnumerateKnownProducers`, `TestDistillGrandfathersAgentSaltedCheckpointEntries` (identity entries and file-era content entries on a checkpoint-backed brain), `TestDistillChunkDoneUnderLegacySalt`.\n- `TestDistillDryRunPredictsCacheAccurately` inverted: a different model now predicts all cached; a changed threshold predicts re-distill.\n- `go test ./internal/cli/` passes.\n\n## Verified on a real brain\n\nDry run with this build after one backfill pass: 147 cached under the default agent and under `--agent codex --effort low` alike. The pre-fix binary reports 0 cached for the same store under its default agent.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n=== PR #426 ===\nauthor: suhaanthayyil\ncreated: 2026-10-21T21:55:40Z\nhead: fix/distill-pipeline -> main\n+562/-3\nfiles: internal/cli/distill_cmd.go, internal/cli/distill_merged_session_reuse_test.go, internal/cli/memory_cmd.go, internal/cli/watch.go, internal/cli/watch_log_prefix.go, internal/cli/watch_log_prefix_test.go, internal/cli/watch_worker_lock_race_test.go\n---BODY---\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.com/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/337\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nThree defects in the distill pipeline, all of which would ship in tonight's release. Closes **COR-2175**, **COR-2177**, **COR-2176**.\n\n## COR-2175 — the daemon blocked itself 🔴\n\nThe watch tick queued long-term reconciliation, launched `memory worker --once` **detached**, then ran a refresh needing the same brain `write.lock`. The worker starts first and holds it 11–16s; refresh's timeout is shorter. The tick logged `brain_locked`, **skipped all agent work including distill**, and did not advance its cursor.\n\n**368 of 369** observed `brain_locked` failures followed a reconciliation queued in the same tick. Any repo with an active session has a changed transcript on nearly every tick — so that repo's daemon distill effectively never ran.\n\nThe watch tick now **waits** for the worker it spawned. Every other caller keeps the detached launch: notify and the session-end hook are done once the work is queued, and blocking them on a 16s worker would slow a fast path for nothing. The wait costs a background tick the worker's runtime — the right trade on a 5m interval.\n\n> The issue offered a cheaper option: *\"skip the worker and let refresh's consolidation do the work.\"* **`refresh.go` contains no consolidation at all**, so that would have silently dropped it. Checked before choosing.\n\n## COR-2177 — distill re-paid for every branch copy 🔴\n\nThe cache is keyed `branch/session`, and session export **deliberately** copies a merged feature-branch session onto the default branch. Every copy was a cache miss with zero chunk reuse, so the same transcript went back to the agent once per branch.\n\nMeasured on `agent-insights`: 69 \"sessions\" were **41 unique**, 19 on more than one branch, `--dry-run` reporting **209 of 209 chunks uncached**.\n\nReuse is now content-addressed across every branch carrying the session. That's sound because the hashes *are* content hashes — an identical chunk has already been through the agent and its facts are already in the store, whichever branch paid. Chunking is prefix-stable, so a copied transcript resolves almost entirely from the union.\n\nThe matcher is **segment-exact**, deliberately. A substring match would let one session's hashes mask another's, and *a chunk wrongly believed distilled is a chunk whose facts never get written* — worse than paying twice. Negative controls cover `abc` vs `abcdef`, and a branch named like a session.\n\n## COR-2176 — watch.log could not be read 🟡\n\nBare `[watch] …` lines: no clock, and on a workspace fan-out no way to tell which repo a line was about. Diagnosing 2175 meant correlating two lines and *guessing* they were the same tick on the same repo — which only worked because one repo dominated that log.\n\nStamped at the **writer**, so a line added later cannot forget it, and **per line** rather than per write so a two-line tick doesn't leave its second line unattributed.\n\n## Verification\n\nVerified by weakening — four guards, each reverted individually:\n\n| weakening | result |\n|---|---|\n| watch tick back to the detached launch | **red** |\n| cross-branch half removed from the reuse decision | **red** |\n| session matcher made a substring | **red** |\n| log stamp blanked | **red** |\n\nOne only became a real guard after a correction: the first chunk-reuse test asserted the helper directly and stayed **green** when the call site was changed to ignore it — the same shape as the bug. The decision is now `distillChunksAlreadyDone`, a value the loop calls, and the test drives that.\n\nFull `internal/cli` suite green, every other package green, `go vet ./...` clean.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"68414e4b-5671-4f3d-b4c1-5576ea5bbefc","timestamp":"2026-10-24T15:08:00.573Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== PR #442 ===\nauthor: karthik-rameshkumar\ncreated: 2026-10-22T10:41:42Z\nhead: claude/inspiring-wright-68270a -> main\n+428/-11\nfiles: internal/cli/watch.go, internal/cli/watch_test.go\n---BODY---\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.com/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/353\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n## Problem\n\nThe supervised watch daemon (`entire-brain workspace watch --distill`, launchd `io.entire.brain-watch.*`) is the only automatic path that runs fact distillation, and on one machine it lost `locks/write.lock` on **576 of 724 ticks over 20 days**. A failed refresh skips the tick's agent work, so the 24h-gated distill ran **3 times in 20 days**.\n\n## Root cause\n\n| Fact | Value | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| Brain write-lock timeout | 10s | `internal/cli/brain.go:33` (`brainWriteLockTimeout`), shared by ~375 `withBrainWriteLock` callers |\n| Holder named in the timeout message | 12s to 25s old at the moment the waiter gave up (201 at 12s, 150 at 13s, 49 at 14s, ...); 21 ticks saw 1m4s to 15m37s | `~/.local/state/entire/logs/watch.log` |\n| Who holds it | the detached `entire-brain memory worker --once` | `locks/write.lock.owner` and `locks/memory-worker.lock.owner` named the same pid (57068); the worker log shows that pass ran 10:07:23Z to 10:09:45Z |\n| Who launched it | the same tick, one step earlier | `watchTick`: delta → reconcile (`reconcileMemoryAndLaunch` → `memoryWorkerLaunch`) → refresh |\n\nThe worker's prepass (`runRefreshDeltaStats` with export: `runExport` and `buildHistoryShortTermLockedContext`, both under write.lock) and its projection publish hold the lock for 12 to 25 seconds on this brain. The tick's heavy refresh asked for the same lock a second or two after launching that worker, waited 10s in session export (\"session export degraded\"), waited 10s again in seed baseline (\"seed baseline failed\", 652 ticks), and aborted the tick. The delta step, which runs *before* the launch, succeeded on 686 of those ticks, which is the tell that the tick was racing its own child.\n\nWhy every tick: the delta reports \"14 transcripts failed to scan\" on 652 ticks, so every tick is a repair tick (reconcile fires, worker launched, heavy refresh runs), and the repair backoff counter only advances on a successful refresh, so it never backed off while the refresh kept losing.\n\nNot part of this: `dirty_worktree` (91 ticks) is the expected refusal while the tree has uncommitted edits (issue #327) and the tick already continues past it.\n\n## Fix (watch.go only)\n\n1. **Launch the worker after the refresh, not before.** The reconcile step is now a deferred closure with a ran-guard (same shape as `runFactsSync`), called explicitly after the refresh outcome on the main path. It still runs exactly once on every exit path (consolidation deferred, no change, refresh failed).\n2. **Bounded retry on lock contention.** `watchRefreshWithLockRetry` re-attempts the free refresh only when the error is a lock timeout (`errFileLockTimeout` or the `brain_locked:` code), up to 3 retries with 15s / 30s / 60s waits, each capped at `--interval`, cancelled by ctx. 105s of waiting total, inside the 5-minute default interval. A 15s first wait outlasts the remainder of a hold that was already 12 to 25s old when the first attempt timed out. The global 10s timeout is deliberately unchanged: it serves interactive commands too.\n3. **Pending gated work survives a lost lock.** If every attempt loses, and an earlier *successful* refresh (within one `--distill-every` window) left distill or seed pending, the tick runs it with the refresh cursor left where it was, so the next tick retries the refresh. This is the rule the no-change path already applies; it covers the minute-plus holders the retry cannot outwait.\n\n## Tests\n\n`go test ./internal/cli/ -run 'Watch'` is green, including nine new tests in `watch_test.go`: retry after a briefly held lock, bounded give-up with the 15/30/60s schedule, no retry for non-lock failures, ctx cancel during a wait, reconcile ordering on success / failure / deferred paths, pending distill running (and not running past the staleness bound) when the lock keeps being lost, the interval cap, and the error classifier.\n\n## Follow-ups (not in this PR)\n\n- Why 14 transcripts fail to scan on every tick: it turns every tick into a repair tick and launches a worker each time.\n- The worker holds write.lock across its whole short-term build in the prepass. Shortening that hold would cut contention for hooks, the MCP server and `brief` as well.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n=== PR #440 ===\nauthor: karthik-rameshkumar\ncreated: 2026-10-22T06:43:02Z\nhead: fix/distill-cache-salt-agent-independent -> main\n+505/-28\nfiles: internal/cli/distill_cache_salt_test.go, internal/cli/distill_cmd.go, internal/cli/distill_cmd_test.go\n---BODY---\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.com/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/351\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\n## Problem\n\nThe distill cache salt hashed the agent, model and effort alongside the prompt, reconcile prompt and threshold. The facts are the product, not the model that produced them, so keying on the model meant the setup backfill (a resolved agent, effort low) and the session-end hook (auto, no effort) never shared a cache entry. Each re-spent on every session the other had already distilled.\n\nOn a real 289-session brain: a bare `entire brain distill --dry-run` reported **0 cached**, while `--agent codex --effort low` reported 72 of the same sessions. No facts had landed from the hook for 16 days.\n\n## Change\n\n- `distillCacheSalt` now covers only what the extractor was asked (prompt with taxonomy, reconcile prompt, confidence threshold) and the preprocessing version.\n- Entries older binaries wrote under the agent-bearing formula are grandfathered against an enumerated set of known producer salts (the current run's own, plus codex and claude-code with and without effort low and a pinned model, in both preprocessing versions) and rewritten under the new salt on first sight. The hook migrates what the backfill wrote and vice versa; nobody has to repeat the writer's flags.\n- The checkpoint fast path, the file-era-to-checkpoint content migration and the per-chunk reuse cache get the same grandfathering.\n- The three salt derivations (current, legacy, legacy agent-bearing) share one prompt/threshold resolution.\n\n## Tests\n\n- `TestDistillCacheSaltIgnoresAgentModelEffort`: salt equality across agent, model, effort and agent command; prompt, reconcile, threshold and preprocessing version still invalidate.\n- `TestDistillGrandfathersAgentSaltedCacheEntries`: seeded legacy entries cost zero agent calls on the backfill run, are rewritten under the new salt, cost zero calls on the hook's configuration, migrate under the hook's configuration too, and changed content still re-distills.\n- `TestLegacyAgentDistillCacheSaltsEnumerateKnownProducers`, `TestDistillGrandfathersAgentSaltedCheckpointEntries` (identity entries and file-era content entries on a checkpoint-backed brain), `TestDistillChunkDoneUnderLegacySalt`.\n- `TestDistillDryRunPredictsCacheAccurately` inverted: a different model now predicts all cached; a changed threshold predicts re-distill.\n- `go test ./internal/cli/` passes.\n\n## Verified on a real brain\n\nDry run with this build after one backfill pass: 147 cached under the default agent and under `--agent codex --effort low` alike. The pre-fix binary reports 0 cached for the same store under its default agent.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n=== PR #426 ===\nauthor: suhaanthayyil\ncreated: 2026-10-21T21:55:40Z\nhead: fix/distill-pipeline -> main\n+562/-3\nfiles: internal/cli/distill_cmd.go, internal/cli/distill_merged_session_reuse_test.go, internal/cli/memory_cmd.go, internal/cli/watch.go, internal/cli/watch_log_prefix.go, internal/cli/watch_log_prefix_test.go, internal/cli/watch_worker_lock_race_test.go\n---BODY---\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.com/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/337\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n\nThree defects in the distill pipeline, all of which would ship in tonight's release. Closes **COR-2175**, **COR-2177**, **COR-2176**.\n\n## COR-2175 — the daemon blocked itself 🔴\n\nThe watch tick queued long-term reconciliation, launched `memory worker --once` **detached**, then ran a refresh needing the same brain `write.lock`. The worker starts first and holds it 11–16s; refresh's timeout is shorter. The tick logged `brain_locked`, **skipped all agent work including distill**, and did not advance its cursor.\n\n**368 of 369** observed `brain_locked` failures followed a reconciliation queued in the same tick. Any repo with an active session has a changed transcript on nearly every tick — so that repo's daemon distill effectively never ran.\n\nThe watch tick now **waits** for the worker it spawned. Every other caller keeps the detached launch: notify and the session-end hook are done once the work is queued, and blocking them on a 16s worker would slow a fast path for nothing. The wait costs a background tick the worker's runtime — the right trade on a 5m interval.\n\n> The issue offered a cheaper option: *\"skip the worker and let refresh's consolidation do the work.\"* **`refresh.go` contains no consolidation at all**, so that would have silently dropped it. Checked before choosing.\n\n## COR-2177 — distill re-paid for every branch copy 🔴\n\nThe cache is keyed `branch/session`, and session export **deliberately** copies a merged feature-branch session onto the default branch. Every copy was a cache miss with zero chunk reuse, so the same transcript went back to the agent once per branch.\n\nMeasured on `agent-insights`: 69 \"sessions\" were **41 unique**, 19 on more than one branch, `--dry-run` reporting **209 of 209 chunks uncached**.\n\nReuse is now content-addressed across every branch carrying the session. That's sound because the hashes *are* content hashes — an identical chunk has already been through the agent and its facts are already in the store, whichever branch paid. Chunking is prefix-stable, so a copied transcript resolves almost entirely from the union.\n\nThe matcher is **segment-exact**, deliberately. A substring match would let one session's hashes mask another's, and *a chunk wrongly believed distilled is a chunk whose facts never get written* — worse than paying twice. Negative controls cover `abc` vs `abcdef`, and a branch named like a session.\n\n## COR-2176 — watch.log could not be read 🟡\n\nBare `[watch] …` lines: no clock, and on a workspace fan-out no way to tell which repo a line was about. Diagnosing 2175 meant correlating two lines and *guessing* they were the same tick on the same repo — which only worked because one repo dominated that log.\n\nStamped at the **writer**, so a line added later cannot forget it, and **per line** rather than per write so a two-line tick doesn't leave its second line unattributed.\n\n## Verification\n\nVerified by weakening — four guards, each reverted individually:\n\n| weakening | result |\n|---|---|\n| watch tick back to the detached launch | **red** |\n| cross-branch half removed from the reuse decision | **red** |\n| session matcher made a substring | **red** |\n| log stamp blanked | **red** |\n\nOne only became a real guard after a correction: the first chunk-reuse test asserted the helper directly and stayed **green** when the call site was changed to ignore it — the same shape as the bug. The decision is now `distillChunksAlreadyDone`, a value the loop calls, and the test drives that.\n\nFull `internal/cli` suite green, every other package green, `go vet ./...` clean.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"ab13f32b-93de-457f-b396-954745d77c9c","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-dp2","root":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-dp2","branch":"fix/distill-pipeline-v2","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/entire-brain","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"entireio","host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/entire-brain","visibility":"public"},{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/entire-brain","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"entirehq/devenv","visibility":"private"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"entireio/entire-brain","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-dp2","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9216a351-aa3d-458a-ab96-8d8c42f30ff0","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"session_id":"80327498-7bd2-42a4-985a-9acba8ccaf85","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-dp2","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","version":"2.1.310","gitBranch":"main","slug":"floofy-discovering-bachman"}
